  "analysis": "Key insight: although `df` reports 'Avail 0' and 100% use on the overlay, my 100MB and 2GB `dd` writes both succeeded and consumed real backing space (Used grew 960G→962G). So the filesystem is actually writable and apt's 'not enough free space' error is a conservative statvfs precheck, not a real hard limit. I have working disk capacity. The blocker is just getting apt/build tools installed. The build itself will need several GB, which appears available. I'll proceed to install build tools.",
  "plan": "Retry the apt-get install of build tools. If apt still refuses due to its disk-space precheck, I'll work around it by downloading the .deb packages directly with wget into /app and installing via dpkg, or bypass the check. Let me first retry the install since disk state may differ now.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends build-essential flex bison libelf-dev libssl-dev wget xz-utils 2>&1 | tail -15\n",
      "duration": 60.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "apt's disk-space precheck keeps failing because statvfs on the overlay reports 0 available. But my direct dd writes succeeded (real space is available). The check is based on the filesystem backing /var/cache/apt/archives. I can bypass the check by mounting a tmpfs over the apt archives directory so statvfs reports real free space there.",
  "plan": "Mount a tmpfs (e.g., 8G) at /var/cache/apt/archives. Then re-run the apt-get install. If mounting fails (no privileges), I'll fall back to downloading debs with wget/apt-get download and dpkg -i. Also check nproc for parallel build and confirm wget works.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mount -t tmpfs -o size=8G tmpfs /var/cache/apt/archives 2>&1; echo \"mount rc=$?\"; df -h /var/cache/apt/archives\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

